Situation:
Falcon Infantry Battalion at FOB Henson and Viper Armored Squadron at COP Ridge are conducting continuous operations and require sustained resupply of critical classes (e.g., ammunition, fuel, rations) over a multi-day window. Supportable stocks are held at Supply Depot Alpha (rear area) and Supply Depot Bravo (airhead). Main Supply Route Copper along the river valley is faster but contested by IEDs and ambush from elevated positions. Alternate Supply Route Flint over high ground is narrower, slower, and weather-sensitive. Air resupply is limited; ground convoys are the primary mode. Convoy vehicles have finite cargo capacity, and some supply classes are bulkier than others, constraining loads. Daily depot release limits apply by supply class. Demands fluctuate by day. Shortfalls are possible but carry an operational penalty representing reduced readiness and combat power.

Mission:
Deliver required loads from depots to supported units across the planning window while minimizing total operational cost, to include transport costs, shortage penalties, per-vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route security risk costs.

Execution/Concept:
- Allocate supply flows from each depot to each supported unit by day and by class within depot release limits. Shortfalls are permitted only at a penalty cost.
- Determine daily which origin–destination routes to activate. Activating a route (sending any vehicles) incurs a fixed Risk Cost that represents escort teams, route clearance, overwatch, and EW support; this overhead applies once per active route per day regardless of the number of vehicles.
- Vehicles may only move on routes that are activated. For each active route, allocate an integer number of vehicles; each vehicle contributes a per-vehicle deployment cost and has a fixed cargo capacity. Loaded volume is constrained by the per-unit volume of each supply class.
- Respect the total fleet cap across all routes each day. Consolidate shipments to reduce fixed risk overhead where feasible; open additional routes only when the combined transport costs, vehicle deployment costs, risk costs, and shortage penalties are improved by doing so.
- Prioritize meeting time-sensitive and mission-critical demands while balancing risk exposure on contested routes. Maintain flexibility to shift sourcing between depots as stocks and route conditions evolve.

